A||||8||GT Bastard||Gearheads, give me some advice....||||4||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||12-01-2000||02:01 AM||||Here's the deal... I've got a '77 Mercedes 280S.  It's a 4 door beast with an inline 6 and a nice body and interior.  I've got a bad #1 cylinder and a tranny with no overdrive anymore.  I've shown it to numerous people and quoted them as low as 600 bucks.  No takers.  I think the car is PIMP and wouldn't mind keeping it, but the drivetrain is not feasible to repair(even if I do the labor myself.)  A mechanic friend and I were talking tonight, and he thinks I need to gut the drivetrain in favor of a smallblock chevy and a TH350 tranny.  He says making mounts would be fairly easy and we could get a custom driveshaft.  Sounds TEMPTING because it's a nice car aside from it's mechanical faults.  We've got TONS of room under the hood, so that's not a problem.  I know a good many things will need to be changed, but it all seems pretty straight-forward.  Has anyone attempted something like this?  I know it's common with older Jags.  Any insight is appreciated.  Thanks...||||reg||4
Z||000001||hugh||12-01-2000||07:27 AM||||If you have that much room,why confine yourself to a small block?Go for a big block with a 450 trans.It shouldn't be any more work.You can probably pick up the whole drivetrain from a junk yard.If it's carbureted there is no wiring to do except the ignition.You would need heavier front springs.One caution,If you did use a big block,the torque would waste the original rear.Good luck if you attempt a change,whatever it is and let us know how it progresses.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||12-01-2000||09:10 AM||||DO IT! DO IT! DO IT! <BR> <A HREF="http://www.jagsthatrun.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jagsthatrun.com/</A> <P>These folks don't mention MBs, but you might find something useful. Or they may be able to steer you in the right direction. I bought their S-10 book. Was *very* detailed.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-01-2000).]||||reg||14
