Z||000006||Will||12-06-2000||01:08 PM||||My father's car was a '67 250 SL Chassis # 108 (sedan).<BR>To get the small block in he had to do two major things:<BR>1) use a Chevy II oil pan with front sump to clear the suspension crossmember.<BR>2) (the hard one) he had to move the starter forward so that the steering idler arm wouldn't hit it.<BR>He used a GM starter with just the shaft and drive, and used a shaft coupler to attach that to the end of a Ford overhung starter so that the whole starter was moved forward by about a foot and attached to the engine mount bracket.<BR>     The chassis mods are very minor. Obviously he had to fabricate his own mount brackets. It can be restored to original condition if you ever decide it would make a better investment than transportation.<BR>     Is yours a 5 spd? Those were RARE!<P>You may call him for more details (It's OK with him).<BR>His name is Hunter and number is 540.652.6263.||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||12-06-2000||09:29 PM||||Thanks for the info... I won't be calling him, but thanks anyways.  The starter issue sounds like a b!tch, but he was using a much older model.  I'm not sure how close they are... Oh well, thanks!||||reg||14
